1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,270
Listen to a talk in an anthropology class.

2
00:00:04,270 --> 00:00:12,120
Today, we're examining the intriguing practice of tattooing in different cultures around the world.

3
00:00:12,120 --> 00:00:15,000
Tattoos have been a form of self-expression

4
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:19,180
and identity for thousands of years varying significantly in meaning

5
00:00:19,180 --> 00:00:21,170
and style across different societies.

6
00:00:21,170 --> 00:00:25,220
In Polynesian cultures, tattoos known as tatau

7
00:00:25,500 --> 00:00:29,467
are deeply rooted in tradition and signify social status,

8
00:00:29,467 --> 00:00:32,060
family heritage, and personal achievements.

9
00:00:32,060 --> 00:00:35,621
These intricate designs often cover large parts of the body

10
00:00:35,621 --> 00:00:40,250
and are applied using traditional methods that have been handed down through generations.

11
00:00:40,250 --> 00:00:44,306
Meanwhile, in Japan, irezumi tattoos are recognized for their bold

12
00:00:44,306 --> 00:00:49,579
and vivid designs and while they were once banned by the Japanese government,

13
00:00:49,579 --> 00:00:53,230
they are now increasingly popular, especially among young people.

14
00:00:53,230 --> 00:00:57,035
Japanese tattoos often depict mythological creatures,

15
00:00:57,035 --> 00:01:04,010
flowers, and scenes from folklore, executed with remarkable precision and detail.

16
00:01:04,010 --> 00:01:09,180
In contrast, Native American tribes use tattoos for spiritual purposes

17
00:01:09,180 --> 00:01:11,050
and as rites of passage.

18
00:01:11,050 --> 00:01:13,910
Their designs, mostly geometric and symbolic,

19
00:01:13,910 --> 00:01:18,200
were believed to provide protection and convey tribal affiliations.

20
00:01:18,200 --> 00:01:24,739
In contemporary Western society, tattoos have evolved into diverse forms of personal expression,

21
00:01:24,739 --> 00:01:28,260
ranging from minimalist designs to elaborate sleeves.

22
00:01:28,260 --> 00:01:31,920
They often represent individuality, memories

23
00:01:31,940 --> 00:01:36,120
or ideologies, reflecting the wearer's unique personality.

24
00:01:36,120 --> 00:01:45,346
Anthropologists are fascinated by how these varying practices of tattooing illustrate the broader themes of cultural identity,

25
00:01:45,346 --> 00:01:48,060
societal norms, and individual expression.

26
00:01:48,060 --> 00:01:52,823
The ongoing study of tattooing practices offers deep insights into human history

27
00:01:52,823 --> 00:01:57,190
and the ways in which we convey meaning through our bodies.
